er, and let $Kc(G, k)$ denote the number of Kempe equivalence classes for the $k$-colorings of $G$. ... This refutes a recent conjecture of Higashitani--Matsumoto.\nTitle: Kempe Classes and Almost Bipartite Graphs\nAuthors: Daniel W. Cranston, Carl Feghali\nDate: Thu Mar 16 14:50:48 2023\n\nLet $G$ be a graph and $k$ be a positive integer, and let $Kc(G, k)$ denote the number of Kempe equivalence classes for the $k$-colorings of $G$. In 2006, Mohar noted that $Kc(G, k) = 1$ if $G$ is bipartite. As a generalization, we show that $Kc(G, k) = 1$ if $G$ is formed from a bipartite graph by adding any number of edges less than $\\binom{\\lceil k/2\\rceil}2+\\binom{\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor}2$. We show that our result is tight (up to lower order terms) by constructing, for each $k \\geq 8$, a graph $G$ formed from a bipartite graph by adding $(k^2+8k-45+1)/4$ edges such that $Kc(G, k) \\geq 2$. This refutes a recent conjecture of Higashitani--Matsumoto.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHamiltonian decompositions of the wreath product of hamiltonian decomposable digraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.13392)\nciteturn3academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; We affirm most open cases of a conjecture that first appeared in Alspach et al. (1987) which stipulates that the wreath (lexicographic) product of two hamiltonian decomposable directed graphs is also hamiltonian decomposable.\nTitle: Hamiltonian decompositions of the wreath product of hamiltonian decomposable digraphs\nAuthors: Alice Lacaze-Masmonteil\nDate: Wed Dec 18 00:08:07 2024\n\nWe affirm most open cases of a conjecture that first appeared in Alspach et al. (1987) which stipulates that the wreath (lexicographic) product of two hamiltonian decomposable directed graphs is also hamiltonian decomposable. Specifically, we show that the wreath product of a hamiltonian decomposable directed graph $G$, such that $|V(G)|$ is even and $|V(G)|\\geqslant 2$, with a hamiltonian decomposable directed graph $H$, such that $|V(H)| \\geqslant 4$, is also hamiltonian decomposable except possibly when $G$ is a directed cycle and $H$ is a directed graph of odd order that admits a decomposition into $c$ directed hamiltonian cycle where $c$ is odd and $3\\leqslant c \\leqslant |V(H)|-2$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Applied Mathematics 325 (2023) 134–151 (https://fileserver-az.core.ac.uk/download/543788549.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; known only partially, and in Section 5.3 we propose a conjecture concerning the structure of minimal hereditary classes ... For both parameters (uniformicity and lettericity), the set of minimal classes is infinite. ... The paper [14] provides a complete description of minimal classes of unbounded distinguishing number, of which\nwith some care, from the results in [11,13,14] and we present it in Section 5.2. For lettericity, such a characterization is\nknown only partially, and in Section 5.3 we propose a conjecture concerning the structure of minimal hereditary classes\nof unbounded lettericity.\nFor both parameters (uniformicity and lettericity), the set of minimal classes is infinite. For uniformicity, we describe\nthis set in two steps starting with a related parameter, known as distinguishing number.\n5.1. Distinguishing number\nThe notion of distinguishing number appeared implicitly, without a name and a formal definition, in [13]. It was given\nits name in [11] and later it was also studied in [10]. Originally, distinguishing number was defined as a parameter\nassociated with classes of graphs. Below we define this parameter with respect to graphs. The two definitions are consistent\nin the sense that the distinguishing number in a class of graphs is either bounded in both definitions or unbounded in\nboth of them.\nGiven a graph G and a set U ⊆V(G), we say that the disjoint subsets U1, ..., Um of V(G) (als